#
# THE COMMIT OF muir THIS ONE IS HELD TO.
#
# Every reference trace in `golden/` is muir's own behavior written down, so
# a commit here means nothing without the commit of muir it was generated
# against: the same generator over a different muir is a different reference,
# and a check that passes against one may fail against the other for reasons
# that have nothing to do with the fabric.  The rule about when to pull ---
# at slice boundaries, never mid-slice --- is discipline where this should be
# a fact in a file.
#
# `make muir-pin` says whether the muir beside this repository is this commit,
# and `make check` runs it and warns.  CI checks this commit out rather than
# muir's main branch, so the pair is tested and not whatever main happens to
# be that morning.
#
# TO MOVE IT: pull muir, write the new SHA here, `rm -f build/*.golden
# build/*.hex`, `make check`, and commit the pin and any trace that changed
# TOGETHER, with what moved in the message.  A trace that changes under a muir
# bump is the most interesting thing that can happen here and must not arrive
# silently in somebody else's commit.
#
# THE `.hex` MATTERS AND THIS LINE USED TO OMIT IT.  `build/boot_prom.hex` and
# `build/sync_prom.hex` are generated out of muir by `golden/src/prom.rs` and
# `golden/src/sync_prom.rs`, but their recipes depend on those `.rs` files and
# `golden/Cargo.toml` alone --- not on muir's source and not on this file.  So
# removing only the goldens leaves both PROM images as the OLD muir made them,
# and every check then runs the old PROM against the new traces without
# saying so.  Removing the images is the remedy until a recipe makes them
# depend on this file, which is the better fix and is not built.
